1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,055
Routing is most of an FPGA's area, and most of bob's.

2
00:00:04,375 --> 00:00:11,118
This chapter follows a signal from a pin, along the wires, through the boxes that join them, to another block.

3
00:00:12,700 --> 00:00:18,554
Between every row and column of tiles runs a channel of wires: 36 tracks wide in bob.

4
00:00:18,874 --> 00:00:24,707
Horizontal wires are called CHANX and vertical ones CHANY, VPR's names.

5
00:00:25,167 --> 00:00:31,333
Each wire is 4 tiles long, so a signal crosses the chip in a few hops instead of one per tile.

6
00:00:31,653 --> 00:00:38,553
And each wire is unidirectional: it has exactly one driver, a multiplexer at the end where it starts.

7
00:00:38,873 --> 00:00:42,368
Some wires run east or north, others west or south.

8
00:00:43,933 --> 00:00:47,848
Where a horizontal and a vertical channel cross sits a switch box.

9
00:00:48,168 --> 00:00:56,124
A wire that ends there can continue straight on, or turn left or right: three choices, which is what Fs equals three means.

10
00:00:56,600 --> 00:01:05,774
Because wires are unidirectional, the box is really a set of multiplexers: each wire leaving it is driven by a mux whose inputs are the wires arriving.

11
00:01:06,094 --> 00:01:12,108
The Wilton pattern changes the track number when a signal turns, so that the network mixes well.

12
00:01:13,667 --> 00:01:16,804
Blocks connect to the channels through connection boxes.

13
00:01:17,124 --> 00:01:26,821
A block input pin is a multiplexer too, but it can only reach a fraction of the tracks: with fc in equal to nought point one, about four of the thirty-six.

14
00:01:27,300 --> 00:01:34,350
Its select value zero means constant zero, one means constant one, and from two upwards it picks a track.

15
00:01:34,670 --> 00:01:41,570
Outputs work the other way round: a block output pin appears as an input on the muxes of a tenth of the tracks beside it.

16
00:01:43,133 --> 00:01:46,596
Every routing multiplexer in bob uses one encoding.

17
00:01:46,916 --> 00:01:49,171
Value zero is constant zero.

18
00:01:49,491 --> 00:01:52,455
On block input pins value one is constant one.

19
00:01:52,775 --> 00:01:59,145
After that the drivers follow in ascending node number, and any value past the last driver also reads zero.

20
00:01:59,600 --> 00:02:02,233
The choice of zero matters more than it looks.

21
00:02:02,553 --> 00:02:08,651
A configuration of all zeros connects nothing to anything, so it cannot contain a combinational loop.

22
00:02:08,971 --> 00:02:14,461
The fabric powers up dark and safe, and JPROGRAM returns it to exactly that state.

23
00:02:16,033 --> 00:02:19,647
Most routing muxes are small: two to twelve drivers.

24
00:02:19,967 --> 00:02:22,509
The tall bar on the right is the crossbars.

25
00:02:22,829 --> 00:02:28,626
Every one of the twenty-four element inputs in each of the hundred blocks chooses among twenty-four sources.

26
00:02:30,200 --> 00:02:35,428
Inside the host, each routing mux is now built by hand from AMD primitives.

27
00:02:35,748 --> 00:02:40,169
Four-input slices of the mux are LUT6s on the two low select bits.

28
00:02:40,489 --> 00:02:44,504
Two of those meet in a MUXF7, and two of those in a MUXF8.

29
00:02:44,824 --> 00:02:48,089
A sixteen-way mux fills exactly one slice.

30
00:02:48,567 --> 00:02:53,411
Left to itself, synthesis had spent nearly a whole LUT on each small mux.

31
00:02:53,731 --> 00:03:02,173
Building them from primitives cut the routing's host LUTs by about thirty percent, and together with the crossbar change it made room for the ten by ten grid.

32
00:03:03,733 --> 00:03:06,461
Here is a real route from the counter example.

33
00:03:06,781 --> 00:03:13,364
The reset button, btn one, enters at a pad on the west edge, at x zero, y four.

34
00:03:13,684 --> 00:03:19,144
Its destination is a logic block at x seven, y three, where the counter lives.

35
00:03:19,600 --> 00:03:28,032
VPR chose 5 wires: down the west channel, east along the bottom on three consecutive length-four wires, then up into the block.

36
00:03:28,352 --> 00:03:33,748
Each hop is one multiplexer, and its configuration value selects the hop before it.

37
00:03:35,300 --> 00:03:38,972
And this is the price of building an FPGA out of another one.

38
00:03:39,292 --> 00:03:47,469
A single routing hop costs up to 4.6 nanoseconds, because it is a host LUT, a MUXF7 and real host wiring.

39
00:03:47,789 --> 00:03:54,601
These three numbers were measured on the routed host design; the LUT, carry and flip-flop figures are still estimates.

40
00:03:55,067 --> 00:04:02,384
VPR now routes using these delays, and chapter six shows how bob turns them into a safe clock for every design.

41
00:04:03,967 --> 00:04:17,234
The mux cell is written by hand and checked against its old behavioural table at every width; every instance is generated; and the fabric testbench routes random netlists and compares every output with the model after every clock.

